Barb=E2=80=99d Comments
https://kicheche.com/kicheche-valley/?utm_source=3DRACK+-+agents+SOW&utm_c=
ampaign=3Df4263899ce-Spot+of+the+Week+Barb%27d+Comments_COPY_05&utm_medium=
=3Demail&utm_term=3D0_-3ea9ca16db-33796176

Bill Westbrook has recently completed his 365 days with us. Sadly it could=
 not be spent with perhaps Kicheche's favourite daughter Barbara=2C who is=
 no longer with us. It took enormous courage to return to his wife=E2=80=
=99s favourite place on earth and his visit affected us all. He wrote to m=
e last night from Queensland and has permitted me to paraphrase some of hi=
s message.

'We had wanted to travel to Kicheche=E2=80=99s Mara camps=2C and to Bush i=
n particular=2C because they were Barb's favourite places in the world.
Firstly I want to acknowledge the absolutely wonderful welcome we received=
 from all at Kicheche.  On our arrival at camp the resourceful Fred Obiri=
 welcomed us=2C not just with the usual fruit drinks=2C but with a heartfe=
lt hug=2C and then he walked me to the usual welcome site with his arm aro=
und me.  And this from a man who is normally efficient but reserved.  I kn=
ew then that my return was going to be just fine.

I was taken aback in the most pleasant way by the extent of the genuine fe=
elings expressed by all about Barb=E2=80=99s passing and the knowledge tha=
t she would not be returning to camp.  Barb was by nature very shy and pre=
ferred that she not be the centre of attention=2C and she would have been=
 completely overwhelmed by the extent of the emotion that her absence caus=
ed.

And that brings me to the welcome I received at Bush Camp which was the ca=
mp at which we spent most time.  Rose and Helen=2C who travelled with me (=
Barb's closest friend and her sister) were aware of my intention to scatte=
r a portion of Barb=E2=80=99s ashes unobtrusively at Bush at some convenie=
nt time during our stay there.  To say that I was overwhelmed (that=E2=80=
=99s an expression that I=E2=80=99ve been using a lot in describing our sa=
fari!) seems inadequate.  I can do no better than to say that the feelings=
 that arose from the welcome we received upon our arrival at Bush were of=
 truly returning home to be among good friends.

https://kicheche.com/kicheche-valley/?utm_source=3DRACK+-+agents+SOW&utm_c=
ampaign=3Df4263899ce-Spot+of+the+Week+Barb%27d+Comments_COPY_05&utm_medium=
=3Demail&utm_term=3D0_-3ea9ca16db-33796176

Dido told me of the staff=E2=80=99s plan to set up a =E2=80=9CGin Bar=E2=
=80=9D out from camp under a favourite Boscia on the following evening at=
 sundowner time. Immediately it occurred that this was the appropriate tim=
e to attend to the scattering of Barb's ashes. The whole occasion was=2C f=
or me=2C a joyous celebration of Barb=E2=80=99s life and the enjoyment tha=
t Kicheche had given her over many years.

So I want to express=2C as best I can in  words=2C my heartfelt thanks to=
 all.  I was and continue to be=2C in retrospect=2C overwhelmed (there I g=
o again) by the wonderful=2C special=2C treatment we received on this rece=
nt journey=2C which was really just a more intense example of the attentio=
n we received whilst in camp over the years=2C and was a part of why we fo=
und it easy to decide to return frequently.'

Thank you Bill. Thank you Barbara. Overwhelming.


=C2=A9 Bill Westbrook

